th1-surjection-curation-001+math1-bijection-curation-001+math1-union-curation-001+math1-proper-curation-001+math1-complement-curation-001+math1-complement-render-001+math1-division-curation-001+math1-reciprocal-curation-001+math1-inequality-curation-001+math1-inequality-feedback-001+math1-maximum-curation-001+math1-minimum-curation-001+math1-boundedset-curation-001+math1-endpoint-curation-001+audit-groups-20261027+topup-math-i-20261028+topup-math-ii-20261028+topup-pt-20261028+topup-math-iii-20261028","concept_id":"Year-1/1b-Math-II-Linear-Algebra/inverse-matrix.html","concept":{"concept_id":"Year-1/1b-Math-II-Linear-Algebra/inverse-matrix.html","questions":[{"id":"la_hq_b8_inverse_matrix_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What two equations must an inverse matrix $A^{-1}$ satisfy?","options":["$A^{-1}+A=I$ and $A-A^{-1}=I$.","$A^{-1}A=A$ and $AA^{-1}=A^{-1}$.","$A^{-1}A=0$ and $AA^{-1}=0$.","$A^{-1}A=I$ and $AA^{-1}=I$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"df24311687917053afdfb021","time_limit":25},{"id":"la_hq_b8_inverse_matrix_008","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which matrix is the inverse of $\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\2&1\\end{pmatrix}$?","options":["$\\begin{pmatrix}1&2\\\\0&1\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\-2&1\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\2&1\\end{pmatrix}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a5b527414aad069e0e439665","time_limit":20},{"id":"la_hq_b8_inverse_matrix_010","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What is $(A^{-1})^{-1}$ for an invertible matrix $A$?","options":["$A$","$I$","$A^{-1}$","$0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"8a89f61d2dca6c24ccd967c3","time_limit":20},{"id":"la_hq_b8_inverse_matrix_011","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why is the order reversed in $(AB)^{-1}=B^{-1}A^{-1}$?","options":["Because matrix inverses are unique.","Because matrix multiplication is commutative.","To undo $AB$, one must undo $B$ first and then $A$.","Because inverses always preserve the original order."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0b738ea00e6dfd6c0947d416","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_hq_b8_inverse_matrix_013","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why does a matrix that loses information fail to have an inverse?","options":["Different inputs can lead to the same output, so the original input cannot be recovered uniquely.","Its entries must all be fractions.","It must have determinant $1$.","It can only be multiplied from the right, so an inverse on the left can never be applied to undo it."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7d126a6c959da9e92250cc87","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_hq_b8_inverse_matrix_014","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"If $\\det(A)=5$, what is $\\det(A^{-1})$?","options":["$1/5$","$5$","$-5$","$0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1716c8bb7e255d399921053f","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_hq_b8_inverse_matrix_015","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Is $\\begin{pmatrix}1&2\\\\2&4\\end{pmatrix}$ invertible?","options":["Yes, because all entries are nonnegative.","Yes, its determinant is $0$.","No, because it is not square.","No, its determinant is $0$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"673f512fd226f409f2a32e49","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_hq_b8_inverse_matrix_018","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"How does invertibility connect to solving $Ax=b$?","options":["If $A$ is invertible, the solution is forced to be $x=bA^{-1}$.","If $A$ is invertible, $Ax=b$ has infinitely many solutions.","If $A$ is invertible, the solution is forced to be $x=A^{-1}b$.","If $A$ is invertible, a solution exists only when $b\\ne0$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"8fd73ea0d53dea14793970b2","time_limit":40},{"id":"la_hq_b8_inverse_matrix_007_curated20261027_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"For $\\boldsymbol{A}=\\begin{pmatrix}2&1\\\\1&1\\end{pmatrix}$, what is $\\boldsymbol{A}^{-1}$?","options":["$\\begin{pmatrix}1&-1\\\\-1&2\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}\\tfrac{1}{2}&1\\\\1&1\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\1&2\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}2&-1\\\\-1&1\\end{pmatrix}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"9b3447e8af413ec8563322a7","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_hq_b8_inverse_matrix_017_curated20261027_002","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For $n\\times n$ matrices $\\boldsymbol{A}$ and $\\boldsymbol{B}$ it is known that $\\boldsymbol{B}\\boldsymbol{A}=\\boldsymbol{I}$. What follows?","options":["$\\boldsymbol{A}\\boldsymbol{B}=\\boldsymbol{I}$ as well, so $\\boldsymbol{B}=\\boldsymbol{A}^{-1}$.","Nothing about $\\boldsymbol{A}\\boldsymbol{B}$ follows; it must still be checked.","$\\boldsymbol{A}\\boldsymbol{B}=\\boldsymbol{I}$ follows only if $\\boldsymbol{A}$ is symmetric.","$\\boldsymbol{B}$ is a left inverse, but $\\boldsymbol{A}$ may still be singular."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"becee4ff99c7320a4369555d","time_limit":40}]}}